it's late 2008 and I'm in Kurdistan Iraq  for a political science field trip we're  driving on a long drive back to the  capital one afternoon when we stopped to  stretch our legs at the base of a gently  sloping grassy hill and as I get out of  the bus I look around to see if there's  a good spot to have that granola bar  I've been saving and something catches  my eye losing out from under the rocks  on the hill are several foot wide  streams of a thick brown mud like  substance and as I approach it to take a  closer look our guide as if he's read my  mind  shouts don't eat that that's oil my  first reaction disgust I was a student  of environmental risk active in  international climate change conferences  I knew for a fact that oil was bad but  as I stared at this foreign brown  substance fascination took over isn't  oil black what was it doing here had an  accident happened seeing this natural  oil seep made me realize that I was  completely out of touch with oil and  from this moment forward oil something  that had seemed so clearly black and  white to me took on many shades of well  brow now stick back to the first time  that you saw oil I bet that it was in a  leak of some sort and that it was bad  news it might have been motor oil  dripping out from under your car more  likely though it was on the news  oil spilling out the side of a tanker  it's downright devastating but all can  do to the environment isn't it those  images of blackened beaches wildlife  drenched in the stuff and battling to  breathe through it I was horrified to  see how in Nigeria forests rivers  livelihoods of thousands have been  poisoned by leaks in over 4,000 miles of  pipelines crisscrossing the Niger Delta  but perhaps freshest in our minds  the BP oil spill from 2010 that sent  over 3.2 million barrels gushing out  into the Gulf of Mexico over the period  of three months now 3.2 million barrels  that's 130 million gallons I'm gonna try  and put that in perspective that's  enough for you guys seated in the back  two rows to get three Starbucks Grande's  every day for the rest of your long  lives and pass that gift card down for a  thousand generations that's a lot that's  would be a lot of coffee now fast  forward a few years and the student of  environmental risk is now a consultant  often often for the oil and gas industry  now in the eyes of many Californians and  certainly many students here working  with or for Big Oil is considered the  single most uncoolest job you could have  it's really the antithesis to being the  coveted tech entrepreneur but it's not  just uncool it's seen as deplorable  total strangers will take it upon  themselves to educate me about my  malevolence and that of my colleagues  well-meaning friends and family will say  is that ethical shouldn't we get you out  of there as if I need rescuing because  I've clearly forgotten to factor in  morality into my daily decisions and on  the one hand I really do appreciate the  honesty but on the other these comments  sting every time why because it's  hypocrisy and lacks nuance where nuance  is true you see on a personal level the  130 million gallons that's built out  during the BP oil spill the thousand  generations worth of Starbucks is a lot  and certainly the destructive power of  that amount of oil is severe but from  the perspective of consumption what's  built out over three months is only an  eighth of what we consume in a day in  the u.s. an eighth and it's not your  neighbors it's us you and I who are the  consumers and as consumers our thirst  for oil is insatiable  even when we see the images on TV but we  don't like to think of ourselves as  consumers so oil remains black to us but  imagine a world without oil in it  there'd be no asphalt some fertilizers  wouldn't exist we'd have no gasoline no  jet fuel and that's already a stretch of  the imagination now try to imagine a  world without plastics everything from  toothbrushes to television screens has  either oil or gas as the critical input  in its production process for me a world  without oil would probably be a lot like  the late 1800s when oil was this new  thing that could make so much happen  much like smart phones are now actually  and smart phones driven by arguably the  john d rockefeller of our times Steve  Jobs have revolutionized the way we  organize ourselves and communicate with  each other certainly I am very thankful  for my art iPhone and I'm very dependent  on it but getting a phone into the hands  of every person on the planet has a  price there are environmental issues  associated with the extraction of the  minerals required and if it's true that  Apple's manufacturer had to hang nets  below its laborers dorms to prevent them  from committing suicide  that doesn't attest to the great labor  conditions in the electronics industry  but are we disgusted when we see the new  iPhone no in fact if we were to meet  someone from Apple at a cocktail party  we'd say something along the lines of Oh  Apple is it I'm a big user of Apple  products myself but we never say Oh  excellent is it I'm a big user of oil  products myself  my point is rarely is anything that  truly rocks the foundation of society  like oil like smartphones all good or  all bad and oil whilst it certainly has  a dark side when the risks manifests  themselves  it still feels the economy literally and  it's not so black and white  for example I'm pretty sure that you  feel great when you can fill up your car  for under $2 a gallon at the pump and  you're not alone because the average  American household will save around $750  this year because of the drop in the oil  price and that's great news but the drop  in oil price also in dangers the 9  million jobs in the oil and gas industry  in the u.s. that's one in 20 jobs a  similar contradiction reduce dependence  on foreign oil is a prominent highlight  that any president would love to boast  with in their State of the Union but in  casual conversation we condemn the shale  gas boom that almost single-handedly got  us this independence whose side is she  on you might be thinking I'm rooting for  accuracy and fairness in debate in a  world of highly polarized media and in a  community we're really taking a stance  is often rewarded I invite you to join  me on this uncomfortable space in the  middle let's go all out on renewable  energy and energy efficiency but also  recognize that oil is really doing  something for us in the meantime let's  hold the oil industry to really high  standards but also respect the people  who work in it because the dismissive  treatment of oil just seems like a form  of hypocrisy when most of us don't  really walk our anti-oil talk are we  really doing all we can to reduce our  oil footprint things like reducing the  amount of plastic we use and waste  fighting for teleconferencing facilities  at our office instead of going for those  much-loved business trips taking  holidays in Big Sur rather than flying  off to New York for the weekend  opting to buy stuff that hasn't been  Arif raided in from the other side of  the planet or driving a more efficient  car if you can't get there by bicycle  but these aren't things that you know  not heard before for me the intermediate  more important step is that we need to  come to terms with our complex difficult  and above all ambiguous relationship  with oil as individual consumers and  that is that oil is a substance that  both creates and destroys many of the  things that we hold dear on complex  issues such as these my hope is that we  go from visceral black-and-white  reactions to responding from a place of  full awareness my hope is that the next  time you see oil you react as an  intrigued consumer and see oil in full  color thank you
 

